 "."
}], "ChatOutput",
 TaggingRules-><|
  "CellToStringData" -> 
   "Below is a **super-complete unified PDE/framework synthesis** you can \
drop into the notebook as the next \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]core equations\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] section for the *RHUFT/MRCE Complete Review \
Laboratory Notebook*.\n\nI\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll organize it as a \
**hierarchical unification**:\n\n1. **Abstract conservation-law master form**\
\n2. **Reaction\[Dash]transport specialization**\n3. **Mass-transport PDE \
framework**\n4. **Coupled-field multiphysics extension**\n5. **Electrostatic \
coupling**\n6. **Boundary/initial condition taxonomy**\n7. **Dimensionless \
structure**\n8. **Wolfram Language implementation templates**\n9. **A unified \
symbolic notebook scaffold**\n\n---\n\n# 1. Master unified field equation\n\n\
A broad unifying form for many continuum models is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial \
\\mathbf{q}}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{q},\\nabla \
\\mathbf{q},\\mathbf{x},t)\n=\n\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{q},\\nabla \
\\mathbf{q},\\mathbf{x},t)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $$\\mathbf{q}$$ = state vector\n\
- $$\\mathbf{F}$$ = flux tensor/vector\n- $$\\mathbf{S}$$ = \
source/sink/reaction forcing\n\nFor a system of \\(m\\) fields:\n\n$$\n\
\\mathbf{q} =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\nq_1\\\\\nq_2\\\\\n\\vdots\\\\\nq_m\n\
\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is the best \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]unified \
equations\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] starting point because it includes:\n\n- \
diffusion\n- advection\n- reaction kinetics\n- electrostatic drift\n- \
multiphysics couplings\n- conservative and nonconservative forms\n\n---\n\n# \
2. Reaction\[Dash]diffusion\[Dash]advection specialization\n\nA standard \
specialization is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{q}}{\\partial t}\n=\n\
\\nabla \\cdot \\big(\\mathbf{D} \\nabla \\mathbf{q}\\big)\n-\n\\nabla \\cdot \
\\big(\\mathbf{v}\\,\\mathbf{q}\\big)\n+\n\\mathbf{R}(\\mathbf{q})\n+\n\
\\mathbf{f}_{\\mathrm{ext}}\n$$\n\nor in conservative form:\n\n$$\n\
\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{q}}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\nabla \\cdot\n\\left(\n\
-\\mathbf{D}\\nabla \\mathbf{q}\n+\n\\mathbf{v}\\,\\mathbf{q}\n\\right)\n=\n\
\\mathbf{R}(\\mathbf{q})+\\mathbf{f}_{\\mathrm{ext}}\n$$\n\nwith:\n\n- \
diffusion tensor $$\\mathbf{D}$$\n- convection velocity $$\\mathbf{v}$$\n- \
reaction term $$\\mathbf{R}$$\n- external forcing \
$$\\mathbf{f}_{\\mathrm{ext}}$$\n\n---\n\n# 3. RHUFT/MRCE-style generalized \
coupled system\n\nIf your notebook is trying to unify many model classes \
under one umbrella, a very useful canonical system is:\n\n$$\n\
\\frac{\\partial q_i}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\nabla\\cdot\n\\left(\n- \
\\sum_{j=1}^{m} D_{ij}(\\mathbf{q},\\mathbf{x},t)\\nabla q_j\n+ \
\\mathbf{v}_i(\\mathbf{q},\\mathbf{x},t)\\, q_i\n+ \
\\mathbf{J}^{\\mathrm{cross}}_i\n\\right)\n=\nR_i(\\mathbf{q},\\mathbf{x},t)\n\
+\n\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^{p} C_{i\\alpha} \\,\\psi_\\alpha\n$$\n\nfor \
$$i=1,\\dots,m$$.\n\nThis adds:\n\n- **cross-diffusion**\n- **field-specific \
convection**\n- **cross-coupled fluxes**\n- **reaction network coupling**\n- \
**auxiliary-field forcing** through $$\\psi_\\alpha$$\n\nThis is often the \
right \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]unified equations\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] form \
if RHUFT/MRCE is meant to be a synthesis framework rather than one single \
model.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Gray\[Dash]Scott-type reaction\[Dash]diffusion \
subsystem\n\nFrom the PDE application pattern shown in \
[NDSolve](paclet:ref/NDSolve), a canonical two-species subsystem is:\n\n$$\n\
\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}\n=\nc_1 \\Delta u - u v^2 + f(1-u)\n$$\n\n$$\
\n\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial t}\n=\nc_2 \\Delta v + u v^2 - (f+k)v\n$$\n\n\
Equivalent conservative-flux form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} + \
\\nabla\\cdot(-c_1 \\nabla u)= f-u(f+v^2)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial \
v}{\\partial t} + \\nabla\\cdot(-c_2 \\nabla v)= -v(f+k-u v)\n$$\n\nThis is a \
good \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]pattern-forming reaction module\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] inside a broader unified framework.\n\n---\n\n# 5. \
Mass transport framework\n\nFor transport of a scalar concentration \
$$c(t,\\mathbf{x})$$, a general mass-transport model is:\n\n$$\n\
\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\nabla\\cdot\n\\left(\n- d \\nabla c + \
\\mathbf{v} c\n\\right)\n=\ns(c,\\mathbf{x},t)\n$$\n\nThis aligns with \
[MassTransportPDEComponent](paclet:ref/MassTransportPDEComponent), which is \
part of the [Mass Transport PDE \
Components](paclet:guide/MassTransportPDEModels) ecosystem within [PDE \
Modeling](paclet:guide/PDEModeling).\n\nFor multiple species:\n\n$$\n\
\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{c}}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\nabla\\cdot\n\\left(\n\
-\\mathbf{D}\\nabla \\mathbf{c}\n+\\mathbf{V}\\mathbf{c}\n\\right)\n=\n\
\\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{c})\n$$\n\n---\n\n# 6. Axisymmetric transport / \
Lamm-type structure\n\nA 1D axisymmetric conservative transport equation, \
inspired by the Lamm-equation example in \
[MassTransportPDEComponent](paclet:ref/MassTransportPDEComponent), can be \
written as:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t}\n+\n\
\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial r}\n\\left[\n\
r\\left(-d\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}+v_r c\\right)\n\\right]\n=0\n$$\n\n\
When:\n\n$$\nv_r = s_c \\omega^2 r\n$$\n\nthe model becomes:\n\n$$\n\
\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial \
r}\n\\left[\nr\\left(-d\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}+s_c\\omega^2 r\\, \
c\\right)\n\\right]\n=0\n$$\n\nThis is a nice \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]radial \
transport module\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] for the unified framework.\n\n---\n\n\
# 7. Electrochemical / boundary-reaction transport\n\nA coupled two-species \
transport model can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial c_A}{\\partial t} \
+ \\nabla\\cdot(-d_A \\nabla c_A)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial \
c_B}{\\partial t} + \\nabla\\cdot(-d_B \\nabla c_B)=0\n$$\n\nwith reactive \
boundary coupling:\n\n$$\n- \\mathbf{n}\\cdot(-d_A \\nabla c_A)= k_c c_A - \
k_a c_B\n$$\n\n$$\n- \\mathbf{n}\\cdot(-d_B \\nabla c_B)= k_a c_B - k_c c_A\n\
$$\n\nThis is structurally consistent with the coupled application form in \
[MassTransportPDEComponent](paclet:ref/MassTransportPDEComponent#1764642656).\
\n\nSo in a unified RHUFT/MRCE sense, one can distinguish:\n\n- **bulk \
evolution equations**\n- **interfacial reaction operators**\n- **far-field \
concentration constraints**\n\n---\n\n# 8. Coupling to electrostatics\n\nA \
natural extension is to couple transport to an electrostatic potential \
$$\\phi$$. Using the electrostatics framework around \
[ElectrostaticPDEComponent](paclet:ref/ElectrostaticPDEComponent), a standard \
field equation is:\n\n$$\n-\\nabla\\cdot(\\varepsilon \\nabla \\phi)=\\rho\n\
$$\n\nFor charged species transport, the flux can be extended to a Nernst\
\[Dash]Planck-type form:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{J}_i\n=\n- D_i \\nabla c_i\n- \\mu_i \
z_i c_i \\nabla \\phi\n+ \\mathbf{v} c_i\n$$\n\nand therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\
\\partial c_i}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf{J}_i\n=\n\
R_i(\\mathbf{c},\\phi)\n$$\n\nwith charge density:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sum_i z_i \
c_i + \\rho_{\\mathrm{fixed}}\n$$\n\nThis gives a powerful unified \
**transport + field + reaction** framework.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Unified \
multiphysics RHUFT/MRCE master block\n\nA strong \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]super \
complete\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] master system is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial \
q_i}{\\partial t}\n+\n\\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf{J}_i\n=\n\
R_i(\\mathbf{q},\\boldsymbol{\\psi},\\mathbf{x},t)\n\\qquad i=1,\\dots,m\n$$\n\
\nwith fluxes\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{J}_i\n=\n- \\sum_{j=1}^{m} D_{ij}\\nabla q_j\n+ \
\\mathbf{v}_i q_i\n- \\chi_i q_i \\nabla \\phi\n+ \
\\mathbf{J}^{\\mathrm{nonlocal}}_i\n$$\n\nand auxiliary fields\n\n$$\n\
\\mathcal{L}_\\alpha(\\boldsymbol{\\psi},\\mathbf{q},\\mathbf{x},t)=0\n\
\\qquad \\alpha=1,\\dots,p\n$$\n\nExamples of $$\\mathcal{L}_\\alpha$$:\n\n- \
electrostatics\n- elasticity\n- heat transfer\n- wave/field equations\n- \
constraint equations\n\nThis form is broad enough to include:\n- reaction\
\[Dash]diffusion systems\n- transport with drift\n- finite-element \
multiphysics\n- interfacial reaction models\n- boundary-driven systems\n\n---\
\n\n# 10. Boundary condition taxonomy\n\nA complete unified framework should \
classify boundaries by **flux**, **value**, and **transfer**.\n\n## \
Dirichlet/value condition\n\n$$\nq_i = g_i\n\\quad \\text{on } \\Gamma_D\n$$\n\
\nIn Wolfram Language this is typically \
[DirichletCondition](paclet:ref/DirichletCondition), and for mass transport \
specifically \
[MassConcentrationCondition](paclet:ref/MassConcentrationCondition), which \
effectively evaluates to a Dirichlet condition.\n\n## Neumann/flux condition\n\
\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}\\cdot \\mathbf{J}_i = h_i\n\\quad \\text{on } \\Gamma_N\n$$\
\n\nFor transport models this corresponds to tools like:\n- \
[MassFluxValue](paclet:ref/MassFluxValue)\n- \
[MassImpermeableBoundaryValue](paclet:ref/MassImpermeableBoundaryValue)\n- \
[MassOutflowValue](paclet:ref/MassOutflowValue)\n- \
[MassTransferValue](paclet:ref/MassTransferValue)\n- \
[MassSymmetryValue](paclet:ref/MassSymmetryValue)\n\n## Robin/transfer \
condition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}\\cdot \\mathbf{J}_i = \\kappa_i(q_i-q_i^{\\ast})\
\n$$\n\nThis is especially important for:\n- inflow/outflow\n- interfacial \
exchange\n- electrode kinetics\n- membrane transport\n\n---\n\n# 11. Initial \
conditions\n\nA fully unified transient model requires:\n\n$$\n\
q_i(0,\\mathbf{x}) = q_{i,0}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nand for auxiliary fields if \
needed:\n\n$$\n\\psi_\\alpha(0,\\mathbf{x}) = \\psi_{\\alpha,0}(\\mathbf{x})\n\
$$\n\n---\n\n# 12. Weak form / FEM-ready structure\n\nFor finite element \
implementation, the conservative PDE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial q}{\\partial t} \
+ \\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf{J} = s\n$$\n\nhas weak form:\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\Omega} \
w \\frac{\\partial q}{\\partial t}\\, d\\Omega\n-\n\\int_{\\Omega} \\nabla w \
\\cdot \\mathbf{J}\\, d\\Omega\n+\n\\int_{\\partial \\Omega} w\\, \\mathbf{n}\
\\cdot\\mathbf{J}\\, d\\Gamma\n=\n\\int_{\\Omega} w s\\, d\\Omega\n$$\n\nThis \
is the conceptual bridge to the finite-element modeling emphasized in [PDE \
Modeling](paclet:guide/PDEModeling).\n\n---\n\n# 13. Dimensionless \
unification\n\nA useful \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]complete review\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] notebook should also include a nondimensional form.\n\
\nLet:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=L\\mathbf{x}' , \\qquad t=T t', \\qquad q_i=Q_i \
q_i'\n$$\n\nThen a transport-reaction equation becomes:\n\n$$\n\
\\frac{\\partial q_i'}{\\partial t'}\n+\n\\nabla' \\cdot\n\\left(\n-\\frac{T \
D_i}{L^2}\\nabla' q_i'\n+\n\\frac{T V_i}{L}\\mathbf{u}_i q_i'\n\\right)\n=\nT\
\\,\\frac{R_i}{Q_i}\n$$\n\nleading to characteristic groups such as:\n\n- **P\
\[EAcute]clet number**:\n  $$\n  \\mathrm{Pe}=\\frac{V L}{D}\n  $$\n- **Damk\
\[ODoubleDot]hler number**:\n  $$\n  \\mathrm{Da}=\\frac{kL^2}{D}\n  \
\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n  \\mathrm{Da}=\\frac{kL}{V}\n  $$\n- **diffusivity \
ratios**\n- **coupling-strength ratios**\n- **reaction selectivity \
parameters**\n\nFor RHUFT/MRCE, this matters because \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]unified frameworks\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] are usually \
most meaningful in dimensionless parameter space.\n\n---\n\n# 14. Symbolic \
Wolfram Language master templates\n\n## A. Generic two-field conservative PDE\
\n\n```wl\neqns = {\n  D[u[t, x, y], t] + \n    Div[-d11 Grad[u[t, x, y], {x, \
y}] - d12 Grad[v[t, x, y], {x, y}] + velU u[t, x, y], {x, y}] == \n    \
reactionU[u[t, x, y], v[t, x, y], x, y, t],\n\n  D[v[t, x, y], t] + \n    \
Div[-d21 Grad[u[t, x, y], {x, y}] - d22 Grad[v[t, x, y], {x, y}] + velV v[t, \
x, y], {x, y}] == \n    reactionV[u[t, x, y], v[t, x, y], x, y, t]\n};\n```\n\
\n## B. Gray\[Dash]Scott form\n\n```wl\neqnsGrayScott = {\n  D[u[t, x, y], t] \
+ Div[-c1 Grad[u[t, x, y], {x, y}], {x, y}] == \n    f - u[t, x, y] (f + v[t, \
x, y]^2),\n\n  D[v[t, x, y], t] + Div[-c2 Grad[v[t, x, y], {x, y}], {x, y}] \
== \n    -v[t, x, y] (f + k - u[t, x, y] v[t, x, y])\n};\n```\n\nThis mirrors \
the application pattern in [NDSolve](paclet:ref/NDSolve#6181865).\n\n## C. \
Mass transport model component\n\n```wl\nvars = {c[t, x, y], t, {x, y}};\n\n\
pars = <|\n  \"DiffusionCoefficient\" -> {{dxx, dxy}, {dyx, dyy}},\n  \
\"MassConvectionVelocity\" -> {vx[x, y, t], vy[x, y, t]},\n  \"ModelForm\" -> \
\"Conservative\"\n|>;\n\neqn = MassTransportPDEComponent[vars, pars] == \
source[x, y, t];\n```\n\nSee \
[MassTransportPDEComponent](paclet:ref/MassTransportPDEComponent).\n\n## D. \
Boundary concentration condition\n\n```wl\nbcDirichlet = \
MassConcentrationCondition[\n  boundaryPredicate[x, y],\n  vars,\n  \
<|\"MassConcentration\" -> cs|>\n];\n```\n\nSee \
[MassConcentrationCondition](paclet:ref/MassConcentrationCondition).\n\n---\n\
\n# 15. Unified notebook-ready architecture\n\nYou could define the \
RHUFT/MRCE framework in the notebook as the following layered schema:\n\n## \
Layer I: State definition\n$$\n\\mathcal{S} = \\{\\mathbf{q}, \
\\boldsymbol{\\psi}, \\Omega, \\partial\\Omega, t\\}\n$$\n\n## Layer II: Bulk \
operators\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}_i[q_i] = \\frac{\\partial q_i}{\\partial t} + \
\\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf{J}_i - R_i\n$$\n\n## Layer III: Flux operators\n$$\n\
\\mathbf{J}_i = \\mathbf{J}_i^{\\mathrm{diff}} + \
\\mathbf{J}_i^{\\mathrm{adv}} + \\mathbf{J}_i^{\\mathrm{drift}} + \
\\mathbf{J}_i^{\\mathrm{cross}}\n$$\n\n## Layer IV: Boundary operators\n$$\n\
\\mathcal{C}_{\\partial\\Omega} = \\mathcal{C}_D \\cup \\mathcal{C}_N \\cup \
\\mathcal{C}_R \\cup \\mathcal{C}_{\\mathrm{interface}}\n$$\n\n## Layer V: \
Coupled auxiliary physics\n$$\n\
\\mathcal{L}_\\alpha(\\boldsymbol{\\psi},\\mathbf{q})=0\n$$\n\n## Layer VI: \
Observation operators\n$$\n\\mathcal{O}[\\mathbf{q},\\boldsymbol{\\psi}]\n$$\n\
\nThese produce:\n- field snapshots\n- integrated mass\n- flux through \
boundaries\n- stability spectra\n- bifurcation maps\n- reduced-order \
observables\n\n---\n\n# 16. Recommended formal \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]unified \
equations\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] statement\n\nIf you want a polished \
statement for the notebook, use this:\n\n> **Unified RHUFT/MRCE field \
framework.**  \n> Let $$\\mathbf{q}=(q_1,\\dots,q_m)$$ denote the principal \
transported/reactive fields and \
$$\\boldsymbol{\\psi}=(\\psi_1,\\dots,\\psi_p)$$ denote auxiliary coupled \
fields. On a domain $$\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb{R}^n$$ over time interval \
$$[0,T]$$, the governing system is\n> $$\n> \\frac{\\partial q_i}{\\partial \
t}\n> +\n> \\nabla\\cdot\\left(\n> -\\sum_{j=1}^{m} D_{ij}\\nabla q_j\n> + \
\\mathbf{v}_i q_i\n> - \\chi_i q_i \\nabla \\phi\n> + \
\\mathbf{J}_i^{\\mathrm{cross}}\n> \\right)\n> =\n> \
R_i(\\mathbf{q},\\boldsymbol{\\psi},\\mathbf{x},t),\n> \\qquad i=1,\\dots,m,\n\
> $$\n> together with auxiliary field equations\n> $$\n> \
\\mathcal{L}_\\alpha(\\boldsymbol{\\psi},\\mathbf{q},\\mathbf{x},t)=0,\n> \
\\qquad \\alpha=1,\\dots,p,\n> $$\n> initial conditions\n> $$\n> \
q_i(0,\\mathbf{x}) = q_{i,0}(\\mathbf{x}),\n> $$\n> and boundary conditions \
chosen from concentration/value, flux, impermeability, transfer, symmetry, or \
outflow classes.\n\nThat is compact, broad, and mathematically respectable.\n\
\n---\n\n# 17. Suggested Wolfram Language scaffold\n\n```wl\nClearAll[\n  q, \
psi, phi, x, y, z, t, vars, pars, eqns, ics, bcs, region, sol\n];\n\nvars = \
{{q1[t, x, y], q2[t, x, y]}, t, {x, y}};\n\npars = <|\n  \
\"DiffusionCoefficient\" -> {{d11, d12}, {d21, d22}},\n  \
\"MassConvectionVelocity\" -> {vx[t, x, y], vy[t, x, y]},\n  \"ModelForm\" -> \
\"Conservative\"\n|>;\n\neqns = {\n  D[q1[t, x, y], t] + \n    Div[\n      \
-d11 Grad[q1[t, x, y], {x, y}] - d12 Grad[q2[t, x, y], {x, y}] + \n      \
{vx[t, x, y], vy[t, x, y]} q1[t, x, y],\n      {x, y}\n    ] == r1[q1[t, x, \
y], q2[t, x, y], phi[t, x, y], x, y, t],\n\n  D[q2[t, x, y], t] + \n    Div[\n\
      -d21 Grad[q1[t, x, y], {x, y}] - d22 Grad[q2[t, x, y], {x, y}] + \n     \
 {vx[t, x, y], vy[t, x, y]} q2[t, x, y],\n      {x, y}\n    ] == r2[q1[t, x, \
y], q2[t, x, y], phi[t, x, y], x, y, t]\n};\n\nics = {\n  q1[0, x, y] == \
q10[x, y],\n  q2[0, x, y] == q20[x, y]\n};\n\nbcs = {\n  \
DirichletCondition[q1[t, x, y] == g1[t, x, y], pred1[x, y]],\n  \
DirichletCondition[q2[t, x, y] == g2[t, x, y], pred2[x, y]]\n};\n\nregion = \
Rectangle[{-1, -1}, {1, 1}];\n```\n\n---\n\n# 18. Best next completion steps\n\
\nTo truly \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]super complete\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] the \
notebook, the next sections should be:\n\n1. **Unified symbol dictionary**\n  \
 - all fields\n   - all coefficients\n   - units\n   - admissible domains\n\n\
2. **Framework assumptions**\n   - continuity\n   - positivity\n   - \
conservation\n   - closure relations\n\n3. **Boundary-condition matrix**\n   \
- Dirichlet / concentration\n   - flux\n   - impermeable\n   - transfer\n   - \
symmetry\n   - outflow\n\n4. **Canonical subclasses**\n   - Gray\[Dash]Scott\n\
   - Lamm/axisymmetric mass transport\n   - coupled boundary-reaction \
transport\n   - electrostatic drift-diffusion\n\n5. **Wolfram implementation \
library**\n   - [NDSolve](paclet:ref/NDSolve)\n   - \
[MassTransportPDEComponent](paclet:ref/MassTransportPDEComponent)\n   - \
[MassConcentrationCondition](paclet:ref/MassConcentrationCondition)\n   - \
electrostatic PDE component workflows\n\n6. **Dimensionless reduction and \
parameter groups**\n\n7. **Verification section**\n   - conservation checks\n \
  - positivity checks\n   - mesh refinement checks\n   - sensitivity studies\n\
