**clean, review-style RHUFT framework** centered on the strongest \
mathematically coherent candidates for a unified continuum formulation, with \
emphasis on transport, reaction, coupling, and boundary logic in Wolfram \
Language.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Review Principles\\n\\n- Keep only **frameworks \
with clear governing equations, boundary logic, and implementation \
pathways**.\\n- Prefer **conservative formulations** when conservation is \
central.\\n- Distinguish carefully between **bulk dynamics**, **flux laws**, \
**source terms**, and **boundary operators**.\\n- Use **built-in Wolfram \
Language PDE functionality** wherever possible.\\n- Organize all candidates \
by **generality**, **interpretability**, **extensibility**, and \
**computational realizability**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## I. Strongest RHUFT \
Candidate Classes\\n\\n### Candidate A. Abstract conservation-law \
framework\\n\\nThe most general candidate is:\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial \
\\\\mathbf{q}}{\\\\partial t} + \\\\nabla \\\\cdot \
\\\\mathbf{F}(\\\\mathbf{q},\\\\nabla \\\\mathbf{q},\\\\mathbf{x},t) = \
\\\\mathbf{S}(\\\\mathbf{q},\\\\nabla \
\\\\mathbf{q},\\\\mathbf{x},t)$$\\n\\nwhere:\\n\\n- $$\\\\mathbf{q}$$ is the \
state vector\\n- $$\\\\mathbf{F}$$ is the total flux\\n- $$\\\\mathbf{S}$$ is \
the source or reaction structure\\n\\n### Logic\\n\\nThis is the strongest \
top-level umbrella because it includes:\\n\\n- diffusion\\n- advection\\n- \
drift\\n- reaction networks\\n- nonlocal closures\\n- coupled auxiliary \
fields\\n\\n### Review status\\n\\n- **Essential master form**\\n- Best for \
theoretical unification\\n- Too abstract by itself for direct model \
closure\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate B. Conservative reaction-transport \
framework\\n\\nA stronger applied candidate is:\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial \
q_i}{\\\\partial t} + \\\\nabla\\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{J}_i = \
R_i(\\\\mathbf{q},\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\psi},\\\\mathbf{x},t)$$\\n\\nwith flux \
decomposition\\n\\n$$\\\\mathbf{J}_i = -\\\\sum_j D_{ij}\\\\nabla q_j + \
\\\\mathbf{v}_i q_i - \\\\chi_i q_i\\\\nabla \\\\phi + \
\\\\mathbf{J}^{\\\\mathrm{cross}}_i$$\\n\\nfor $$i=1,\\\\dots,m$$.\\n\\n### \
Logic\\n\\nThis is the strongest practical RHUFT candidate because it cleanly \
separates:\\n\\n- diffusion or cross-diffusion\\n- convection\\n- \
field-driven drift\\n- cross-coupled fluxes\\n- reaction or conversion \
terms\\n\\n### Review status\\n\\n- **Primary recommended RHUFT core**\\n- \
Broad enough for most continuum multiphysics transport models\\n- Compatible \
with finite-element workflows\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate C. Mass transport \
PDE framework\\n\\nFor a scalar concentration \
$$c$$:\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial c}{\\\\partial t} + \
\\\\nabla\\\\cdot\\\\left(-d\\\\nabla c + \\\\mathbf{v}c\\\\right) = \
s(c,\\\\mathbf{x},t)$$\\n\\nThis is directly aligned with \
[MassTransportPDEComponent](paclet:ref/MassTransportPDEComponent).\\n\\n### \
Logic\\n\\nThis is the strongest built-in implementation candidate for \
transport-centered RHUFT variants.\\n\\n### Review status\\n\\n- \
**Implementation-critical**\\n- Excellent for clean Wolfram Language \
realization\\n- Should be treated as a canonical computational specialization\
\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate D. Coupled reaction-diffusion systems\\n\\nFor \
example:\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial u}{\\\\partial t} = \
\\\\nabla\\\\cdot(d_u\\\\nabla u) + \
f_u(u,v,\\\\dots)$$\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial v}{\\\\partial t} = \\\\nabla\
\\\\cdot(d_v\\\\nabla v) + f_v(u,v,\\\\dots)$$\\n\\n### Logic\\n\\nThese are \
strong candidates when RHUFT is interpreted as a unifying framework for \
pattern formation, kinetics, and field evolution.\\n\\n### Review \
status\\n\\n- Strong subclass\\n- Important but not broad enough to be the \
sole RHUFT definition\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate E. Drift-diffusion plus \
electrostatic coupling\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial c_i}{\\\\partial t} + \
\\\\nabla\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf{J}_i = R_i$$\\n\\n$$\\\\mathbf{J}_i = \
-D_i\\\\nabla c_i - \\\\mu_i z_i c_i\\\\nabla \\\\phi + \\\\mathbf{v}c_i$$\\n\
\\n$$-\\\\nabla\\\\cdot(\\\\varepsilon\\\\nabla\\\\phi)=\\\\rho$$\\n\\n### \
Logic\\n\\nThis is one of the strongest extensions if RHUFT is meant to cover \
charged transport or field-mediated migration.\\n\\n### Review status\\n\\n- \
Strong multiphysics extension\\n- Recommended as a second-tier core module\\n\
\\n---\\n\\n## II. Best Unified RHUFT Recommendation\\n\\nThe strongest \
complete RHUFT framework candidate is:\\n\\n> Let \
$$\\\\mathbf{q}=(q_1,\\\\dots,q_m)$$ denote the primary fields and \
$$\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\psi}=(\\\\psi_1,\\\\dots,\\\\psi_p)$$ denote auxiliary \
coupled fields. Then on $$\\\\Omega\\\\subset\\\\mathbb{R}^n$$,\\n>\\n> \
$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial q_i}{\\\\partial t} + \
\\\\nabla\\\\cdot\\\\left(-\\\\sum_{j=1}^{m} D_{ij}\\\\nabla q_j + \
\\\\mathbf{v}_i q_i - \\\\chi_i q_i\\\\nabla\\\\phi + \
\\\\mathbf{J}_i^{\\\\mathrm{cross}}\\\\right) = \
R_i(\\\\mathbf{q},\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\psi},\\\\mathbf{x},t), \\\\qquad \
i=1,\\\\dots,m,$$\\n>\\n> together with auxiliary equations\\n>\\n> \
$$\\\\mathcal{L}_\\\\alpha(\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\psi},\\\\mathbf{q},\\\\mathbf{x}\
,t)=0, \\\\qquad \\\\alpha=1,\\\\dots,p.$$ \\n\\n### Why this is strongest\\n\
\\n- preserves conservation structure\\n- accommodates multiple transport \
mechanisms\\n- allows field coupling\\n- subsumes reaction-diffusion and \
mass-transport models\\n- supports clean boundary classification\\n- maps \
well to [NDSolveValue](paclet:ref/NDSolveValue) and PDE component \
workflows\\n\\n---\\n\\n## III. Boundary Logic Review\\n\\nFrom the mass \
transport framework, the main boundary classes are:\\n\\n- \
[DirichletCondition](paclet:ref/DirichletCondition)\\n- \
[NeumannValue](paclet:ref/NeumannValue)\\n- \
[PeriodicBoundaryCondition](paclet:ref/PeriodicBoundaryCondition)\\n\\nThe \
mass transport tutorial identifies four major boundary classes:\\n\\n1. \
concentration or value type\\n2. flux type\\n3. Robin or transfer type\\n4. \
periodic type\\n\\nSee [Mass Transport \
tutorial](paclet:PDEModels/tutorial/MassTransport/MassTransport).\\n\\n### \
Clean RHUFT boundary taxonomy\\n\\n#### Value condition\\n\\n$$q_i = g_i \
\\\\quad \\\\text{on } \\\\Gamma_D$$\\n\\nUse \
[DirichletCondition](paclet:ref/DirichletCondition), or for transport \
concentrations, \
[MassConcentrationCondition](paclet:ref/MassConcentrationCondition).\\n\\n####\
 Flux condition\\n\\n$$\\\\mathbf{n}\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf{J}_i = h_i \\\\quad \\\
\\text{on } \\\\Gamma_N$$\\n\\nUse [NeumannValue](paclet:ref/NeumannValue), \
or transport-specific wrappers such as \
[MassFluxValue](paclet:ref/MassFluxValue).\\n\\n#### Impermeable condition\\n\
\\nFor the conservative transport \
model:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathbf{n}\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf{J}=0$$\\n\\nThe transport \
tutorial notes that for the conservative form, this is effectively a zero \
Neumann condition and can be implicit.\\n\\n#### Transfer or Robin \
condition\\n\\nA standard surrounding-flux form \
is:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathbf{n}\\\\cdot(d\\\\nabla \
c)=k(c_{\\\\mathrm{ext}}-c)$$\\n\\nThis can be represented by \
[MassTransferValue](paclet:ref/MassTransferValue).\\n\\n#### Outflow \
condition\\n\\nOutflow handling depends on the model form. The distinction \
between conservative and nonconservative formulations is important in \
transport models.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## IV. Conservative vs Nonconservative \
Logic\\n\\nThis distinction is critical and should remain explicit in the \
RHUFT review.\\n\\nFor mass transport, the documentation states:\\n\\n- in \
the **conservative formulation**, [NeumannValue](paclet:ref/NeumannValue) \
specifies the boundary value of $$\\\\mathbf{n}\\\\cdot(d\\\\nabla c - \
\\\\mathbf{v}c)$$\\n- in the **nonconservative formulation**, \
[NeumannValue](paclet:ref/NeumannValue) specifies the boundary value of \
$$\\\\mathbf{n}\\\\cdot(d\\\\nabla c)$$\\n\\nThis is one of the most \
important review points for keeping the notebook logically correct.\\n\\n### \
Practical rule\\n\\n- use the **conservative form** when conservation and \
true total flux accounting are central\\n- use the **nonconservative form** \
only with explicit awareness of how convective boundary contributions are \
handled\\n\\n---\\n\\n## V. Strongest Framework Ranking\\n\\n### Tier 1: Core \
RHUFT candidates\\n\\n1. **Conservative multi-field reaction-transport \
system**\\n2. **Abstract conservation-law master equation**\\n3. **Mass \
transport PDE specialization**\\n\\n### Tier 2: Major extension \
modules\\n\\n4. **Electrostatic drift-diffusion coupling**\\n5. **Coupled \
reaction-diffusion systems**\\n6. **Interface-reaction or boundary-reaction \
transport systems**\\n\\n### Tier 3: Special geometries and reduced \
forms\\n\\n7. axisymmetric transport forms\\n8. radial transport models\\n9. \
application-specific reduced closures\\n\\n---\\n\\n## VI. Clean Wolfram \
Language Framework Templates\\n\\n### 1. Abstract conservative PDE \
template\\n\\n```wl\\nClearAll[q, x, y, t, flux, source];\\n\\npde = D[q[t, \
x, y], t] + Div[flux[t, x, y], {x, y}] == source[t, x, y];\\n```\\n\\n### 2. \
Transport specialization\\n\\n```wl\\nClearAll[c, x, y, t, d, vx, vy, \
reaction];\\n\\npde = D[c[t, x, y], t] + \\n   Div[-d Grad[c[t, x, y], {x, \
y}] + {vx[t, x, y], vy[t, x, y]} c[t, x, y], {x, y}] == \\n   reaction[c[t, \
x, y], x, y, t];\\n```\\n\\n### 3. Built-in mass transport \
component\\n\\n```wl\\nvars = {c[t, x], t, {x}};\\n\\npars = <|\\n   \
\\\"DiffusionCoefficient\\\" -> 0.026,\\n   \\\"MassConvectionVelocity\\\" -> \
{0.1},\\n   \\\"ModelForm\\\" -> \\\"Conservative\\\"\\n|>;\\n\\nfluxBc = \
MassFluxValue[x == 0, vars, pars, <|\\\"MassFlux\\\" -> 1|>];\\nconcBc = \
MassConcentrationCondition[x == 1, vars, pars, <|\\\"MassConcentration\\\" -> \
0|>];\\n\\neqn = MassTransportPDEComponent[vars, pars] == \
fluxBc;\\n```\\n\\nThis matches the structure documented for \
[MassTransportPDEComponent](paclet:ref/MassTransportPDEComponent) and \
[MassFluxValue](paclet:ref/MassFluxValue).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## VII. Review \
Conclusions\\n\\n### Most complete RHUFT framework\\n\\nThe cleanest and \
strongest complete candidate is:\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\partial q_i}{\\\\partial \
t} + \\\\nabla\\\\cdot\\\\mathbf{J}_i = \
R_i$$\\n\\nwith\\n\\n$$\\\\mathbf{J}_i = -\\\\sum_j D_{ij}\\\\nabla q_j + \
\\\\mathbf{v}_i q_i - \\\\chi_i q_i\\\\nabla\\\\phi + \
\\\\mathbf{J}^{\\\\mathrm{cross}}_i$$\\n\\nplus auxiliary coupled \
equations\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal{L}_\\\\alpha(\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\psi},\\\\mathbf{\
q})=0.$$ \\n\\n### Why this should anchor the notebook\\n\\n- it is \
mathematically clean\\n- it is physically interpretable\\n- it contains the \
strongest candidate subclasses\\n- it supports a proper review structure\\n- \
it keeps boundary logic explicit\\n- it is extensible without becoming \
vague\\n\\n---\\n\\n## VIII. Next Clean Sections to Add\\n\\n1. Symbol \
dictionary and units\\n2. Boundary-condition comparison table\\n3. \
Conservative vs nonconservative derivation notes\\n4. Canonical subclasses \
and when to use each\\n5. Wolfram Language implementation patterns\\n6. \
Verification and validation checklist\\n\\n---\\n\\n## IX. Final Notebook \
Position\\n\\n**Recommended final RHUFT review position:**\\n\\nUse the \
**conservative multi-field reaction-transport framework** as the principal \
RHUFT definition, and treat all other strong candidates as subclasses, \
reductions, or coupled extensions.\"\>",
