formulation**, [NeumannValue](paclet:ref/NeumannValue) specifies the boundary \
value of $$\\mathbf{n}\\cdot(d\\nabla c)$$\n\nThis is one of the most \
important review points for keeping the notebook logically correct.\n\n### \
Practical rule\n\n- use the **conservative form** when conservation and true \
total flux accounting are central\n- use the **nonconservative form** only \
with explicit awareness of how convective boundary contributions are handled\n\
\n---\n\n## V. Strongest Framework Ranking\n\n### Tier 1: Core RHUFT \
candidates\n\n1. **Conservative multi-field reaction-transport system**\n2. \
**Abstract conservation-law master equation**\n3. **Mass transport PDE \
specialization**\n\n### Tier 2: Major extension modules\n\n4. **Electrostatic \
drift-diffusion coupling**\n5. **Coupled reaction-diffusion systems**\n6. \
**Interface-reaction or boundary-reaction transport systems**\n\n### Tier 3: \
Special geometries and reduced forms\n\n7. axisymmetric transport forms\n8. \
radial transport models\n9. application-specific reduced closures\n\n---\n\n\
## VI. Clean Wolfram Language Framework Templates\n\n### 1. Abstract \
conservative PDE template\n\n```wl\nClearAll[q, x, y, t, flux, source];\n\n\
pde = D[q[t, x, y], t] + Div[flux[t, x, y], {x, y}] == source[t, x, y];\n```\n\
\n### 2. Transport specialization\n\n```wl\nClearAll[c, x, y, t, d, vx, vy, \
reaction];\n\npde = D[c[t, x, y], t] + \n   Div[-d Grad[c[t, x, y], {x, y}] + \
